## 🚨 Signs Your Quartz Watch Battery Needs Replacing

Leaving a completely dead battery inside your watch for extended periods can cause **electrolyte leakage**, which corrodes the circuit board and movement gears. Watch for these warning signs:

1. **The End-of-Life (EOL) Indicator:** Many modern quartz movements feature an EOL function. When the battery voltage drops, the second hand begins **jumping in 2-second or 4-second intervals** instead of ticking every second. It still keeps accurate time, but alerts you to replace the battery soon.
2. **Loss of Time Accuracy:** If your quartz watch starts lagging by minutes or hours per day, the battery voltage is likely dropping below the threshold needed to drive the motor consistently.
3. **Chronograph / Backlight Failure:** High-drain complications like stopwatch timers, digital backlights (e.g., Casio Illuminator), or alarms stop functioning before the main hands stop.

